<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  Enables ClickHouse's native BACKUP/RESTORE statements (audit F-04).

  Without this file, `BACKUP DATABASE default TO File(...)` fails with
  "Path ... is not allowed for backups" — ClickHouse refuses to write a backup
  anywhere that has not been explicitly declared. That refusal is deliberate on
  ClickHouse's part: BACKUP takes a server-side filesystem path, so an
  unrestricted setting would let any user with query access write arbitrary
  files as the ClickHouse user.

  WHY File() AND NOT Disk(): a Disk-based backup destination is the better shape
  at Tier 2+, where the target is S3 and ClickHouse writes there directly. At
  Tier 1 the target is the local NVMe and then an off-host copy made by
  scripts/backup.sh, so a plain path is simpler and one moving part fewer. The
  disk-based form is the thing to revisit when ClickHouse gets S3 external
  disks (CLAUDE.md §8.3), not before.

  ⚠️ The path below lives INSIDE /var/lib/clickhouse, which is the
  clickhouse_data volume. That is intentional — it is the only writable
  persistent path the container already owns — but it means a backup consumes
  the same NVMe budget as the data it protects. scripts/backup.sh therefore
  removes the local ClickHouse backup as soon as it has been archived and
  copied off-host. If that cleanup is ever skipped, the disk-exhaustion risk in
  CLAUDE.md §10.1 gets worse, not better, so the alert on projected
  days-until-full covers this too.
-->
<clickhouse>
    <backups>
        <allowed_path>/var/lib/clickhouse/backups/</allowed_path>
    </backups>
</clickhouse>
